form who  refused to come to school prepare for  our kids  i had concerns with the major problem  with locke was frank wells in order to  even get a teacher  to be seriously considered for  contamination you have to have three  years of perfect  documentation  on their insufficiencies  and that's just the beginning of the  process it can take possibly a hundred  plus years to fire all the bad teachers  turns out it takes much less time to get  rid of some principles  we were calling for frank's removal for  for a couple years in may the los  angeles unified school district  stripped frank wells of his duties the  district send three police officers and  an associate superintendent of schools  to  the school site to escort me off campus  and demand my keys now how could that  happen  well says that it was payback for his  support of a bold plan that would have  yanked control of lock high school away  from the district and the teachers union  the plan was inspired by this guy steve  barr i had a dinner with frank wells  we hit it off green dot had a history  of success in its schools with like  population green dot is a non-profit  organization headed by barr it runs 10  charter schools in some of la's poorest  areas  we go into areas  you know very simply that have sixty  seventy percent dropout rates and we  reverse that achievement gap we retain  and graduate eighty ninety percent of  the kids that come in charter schools  are public schools run by private  companies or non-profits like green dot  teachers have more control over how they  teach students and principals have more  control over things like who gets hired  decisions are made at the school site  not by administrators at district  headquarters  you've got thirty thousand people who  work at l unified for example that don't  work at school sites they're not  teachers they're not support people for  teachers  barr's office overlooks district  headquarters but he's not interested in  paying for a skyscraper or lots of  administrators instead he pays teachers  more  i want all the money at the school site  in the classrooms paying the best  teachers giving them the best tools at  green dot students are held to high  standards and schools are kept small to  give kids personalized attention the  idea is to borrow what works in the most  expensive private schools you would  never spend 25 grand to send your kid to  a private school  that's five thousand seats or four  thousand seats it'd probably be three or  four or five hundred there were the  number of key things that appealed to me  about the green dot approach there were  safety nets after school tutoring  saturday school and things of that sort  vicki reyes thought the green dot  approach could help turn lock around why  not try it when something's not working  in your life and you know you're trying  to do something a certain way you always  stop and say well let me start over and  do it you know let's try another  approach 11th grader nyla koich attends  green dot's oscar de la hoya school  she transferred from a traditional  public school but still keeps in touch  with friends there if i tell them  something like oh i'm learning about  this about that like oh how could you  get that it's like really hard and i'm  like well not really just like is how  the teachers explain it and everything  nila says there were lots of gangs in  her old school but not here  there's no gangs here it's really hard  for us to have a gang if anybody wanted  one and i don't think anybody would want  one because everybody's like  everybody knows each other well  at first nyla didn't want to transfer  schools but now she's glad her mom  convinced her to give green dot a shot  it's it's really sad that these parents  have been  manipulated by a  snake oil salesman like steve barr but  steve barr has plenty of critics  who  feeds at the troft of public education  and is nothing  but a  vampire for instance uni leaders see his  charter schools as a threat to the  traditional public school system private  schools and charter schools can cream  from the top they can also get rid of  any child that they want to public  school can't we never kick kids out of  school  actually public schools can opportunity  transfer and kick kids out of school  our mission is we never do that his  model disrespects teachers i'll give up  on a on a teacher faster than a 14 year  old kid they lose a lot of their rights  as  as teachers we don't build the schools  for the teachers that's the big  disconnect you saw in the board meeting  you know the president of the teachers  you just walk up and take over a  microphone take this anymore we have a  set of four demands that must be met  bizarre  you know parent would do that they would  they would take them out like in about  you know five words these demands are  immutable ucla have a lot to lose  because they will lose membership  membership means you lose money you lose  money you lose influence but plenty of  lock teachers don't side with the union  many joined with wells and pushed for a  first of its kind reform they wanted  lock to break free from the district and  transform into a cluster of small green  dot schools to do that they would need a  majority of lock teachers to sign on to  the plan within three or four hours the  teachers went and  after school got the majority of the  teachers to sign this thing and lock  high school was in play  that's when the district struck back and  turned principal wells into former  principal wells  vicki reyes couldn't believe it it was  terrible and it had an impact on the  children which made the children kind of  like go into a frenzy they just went  crazy the um school had a riot the next  day this is somebody that finally cares  about them when they was taking dr wells  off the campus i i started crying  because i felt like what is this school  going to be without him for the rest of  the year wells was ordered to report t